From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hunter B Hough <git@hough.ink>
Date: Sun, 13 Sep 2026 16:25:41 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 093/103] fix(reap): renew every gate at the point of use and
 refuse a removal on a spent clock (gcp-jxtc)
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

gcp-jxtc is the bead gating re-arming the pack worktree reaper. It was
written against the city pin, which still carries the `git worktree
remove --force` plus unbounded `rm -rf "$WT"` fallback; that shape is
already gone from integration — gcp-mves replaced the whole sequence with
rename-prune-delete, so the timeout-vs-refusal ambiguity has no call site
left and the kill-safety and detectability halves of the standing
condition are met. What was NOT met is the second required change:
`revalidate_gates` renewed the clean-tree, bead-status and owner gates at
the point of use, but carried two others over on aged evidence.

Gate 1, the path, is now re-checked first. It is the gate every other one
is about — a `git status` verdict is about whatever is at the path now,
not about what was enumerated — and it is the cheapest check in the
script, so nothing was bought by trusting it. A witness salvage, an
operator, or another housekeeping pass can move or replace a directory
mid-cycle, and a symlink left in its place would have the delete follow
itself straight out of the tree every gate was evaluated against. Refusal
records `worktree_shape_unconfirmed` / `shape_changed_at_point_of_use`.

Gate 5, publication, is now renewed on the no-such-bead route rather than
returned past. That route has no bead closure to authorise the reap and
no owner to confirm absent, so "the commits exist somewhere other than
this directory" is the whole of its safety case — which makes it the last
evidence that should be allowed to age, not the first. `recheck_publication`
is deliberately narrower than the deciding probe it mirrors: it is only
re-confirming, so the three unknowns collapse into one token and anything
short of a renewed `yes` is a refusal.

The bead's remaining requirement — a destructive step must not start
unless enough budget remains to finish it — lands as `--removal-reserve`
(default 2s, `GC_REAP_REMOVAL_RESERVE_SECONDS`). The removal is kill-safe,
not kill-free: an interruption leaves a `.reaping` directory for the next
cycle to finish and a line for the witness to read, and entering one with
no clock left chooses that for nothing when the reap is idempotent.
Short of the reserve the candidate is recorded `worktree_reap_failed` /
`reap_budget_insufficient` — authorised, not performed, nothing renamed
and nothing removed — and held inside the next cycle's window rather than
rotated past, like every other truncation.

Validation: `bash gastown/tests/test_polecat_worktree_reap.sh` (28 tests,
green), `shellcheck -S warning` clean on both files. Four tests added,
including the bead's two named acceptance cases: a bead that reopens
between classification and removal is refused (`GC_BD_REOPEN_AFTER` serves
`closed` to the classification read and `open` to the re-check read), and
a removal that cannot be finished within the budget deletes nothing and
emits `worktree_reap_failed`. The two new gates were confirmed red against
the pre-change script; the reopen test passes against it, which is the
point — it is regression coverage for an invariant that had none.

Not in scope per the bead's Do NOT: the reaper stays in dry-run. Arming it
with --no-dry-run remains a separate, explicitly-gated step and Hunter's
call; the promotion criterion in agents/witness/agent.toml is untouched.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
